title: Differentiated Thyroid Carcinoma
---

# Differentiated Thyroid Carcinoma

- **Pathology and Histopathology** - growth is TSH-dependent:
  - <u>Papillary carcinoma</u> (90% of irradiation induced thyroid CA) - often multifocal and spread to regional LN (cervical)
  - <u>Follicular carcinoma</u> (10-20%) - often focal encapsulated region and rarely spread to regional LN, but metastasis to bone lungs and brain via haematogenous route
- **Mx:**
  - **Thyroid surgery** - treatment and histological Dx:
    - <u>Extent of thyroidectomy</u>
    - <u>Extent of neck dissection</u>
  - **Adjuvant radioiodine (I131) ablation** - low-dose (30mCi) equally as effective as large dose (100 mCi)
  - **Long term thyroxine replacement** - levothyroxine:
    - <u>Regimen</u> - 100-200 mcg daily (dose sufficient to suppress TSH to preven recurrence)
- **Follow-up:**
  - **Serial measurement of TG:**
    - <u>Rationale</u> - should be not measurable in patients undergone total thyroidectomy and on suppressive doses of levothyroxine
    - <u>Elevated TG</u> - suggestive of recurrence or distant metastasis localised by USG, CT, MRI or thyroid scitiography
  - **Subsequent scintigraphy and radioiodine ablation for suspected recurrence:**
    - <u>Requirements</u> - TSH \> 20 mU/L, achieved by:
      - Withdrawal of suppressive levothyroxine for 4-6 weeks (transient symptomatic hypothyroidism)
      - IM injection of recombinant TSH (expensive)
- **Prognosis** - currative with appropriate Tx (\> 90% 10y survival)
